2. Specification neutrality
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The normative specification is intentionally vendor-neutral and
form-factor agnostic. "IntentGrant" refers to the protocol, not to any
particular product, vendor, or hardware form. A confirmation endpoint
may be implemented as a ring, watch, band, glasses, or a physical
button — whichever fits the scenario.

IntentGrant is designed as an implementation profile aligned with
existing and emerging standards:

  - RFC 9396 (OAuth 2.0 Rich Authorization Requests)
  - IETF Secure Intent Protocol (draft)
  - OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange (RFC 8693)
  - OAuth 2.0 DPoP (RFC 9449) / mTLS (RFC 8705)

It is not a competing proprietary protocol.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Implementation status — READ BEFORE EVALUATING
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT: All capability claims in this repository are graded in
Chapter 12 ("Implementation Status Statement") of the normative
specification, using three levels:

  - Implemented (prototype)
  - Design intent — depends on the mass-production secure version
  - Future work

Claims relating to secure-element (SE) chip signing, physical tamper
response, "private key never leaves the chip", and biometric binding
are DESIGN INTENT at the prototype stage. They are NOT to be
represented as implemented capabilities.

Readers, reviewers, and evaluators must rely on Chapter 12 as the
authoritative statement of current scope. A working prototype
demonstrates execution capability; it does not constitute commercial
validation.

